// spec: a declared operator!= that returns bool is an error, a member or a free function, written, defaulted or a template, used or not (kovarex, 2026-11-09): `a != b` is always `!(a == b)` (C++20's rewrite, which kpp does), so a written one can only repeat == or disagree with it; an operator!= returning another type (a SIMD mask) stays allowed; a class with only == still compares with != (docs/differences-from-cpp.md, "`!=` is always `!(a == b)`")
